  "verdicts": [
    {
      "id": "P1",
      "claim": "the instrument has the corpus it describes: 203 rules, 13,578 injections, both halves deep enough to compare rules",
      "observed": "203 rules, 13578 injections, 29 databases per half, 135 rules seen five times in each",
      "holds": true
    },
    {
      "id": "P2",
      "claim": "rule records are more dispersed than the pack structure alone can explain",
      "observed": "chi2 293.3 against a null mean of 209.6 and a 99th percentile of 280.4, p = 0.0047",
      "holds": true
    },
    {
      "id": "P3",
      "claim": "the dispersion does not replicate: a rule measured on one set of databases is not the same rule on another",
      "observed": "r = -0.134 over 135 rules, sign agreement 49.1% over 53",
      "holds": true
    },
    {
      "id": "P4",
      "claim": "the ledger is not reliable enough to prune on",
      "observed": "Spearman-Brown reliability -0.309 at full length",
      "holds": true
    },
    {
      "id": "P5",
      "claim": "a rule that looked harmful does not keep looking harmful where it was not measured",
      "observed": "flagged 58 rules covering 2545 questions at -0.55pp against -0.49pp clean, difference -0.06pp [-1.97pp, +1.56pp]",
      "holds": true
    },
    {
      "id": "P6",
      "claim": "a usable ledger is out of reach at this unit of memory",
      "observed": "670 injections per rule (10x the measured 66.9); at a split-half correlation of -0.13 no multiple reaches 0.8",
      "holds": true
    },
    {
      "id": "P7",
      "claim": "the pack-size lead is corpus-dependent and does not license a decision",
      "observed": "+4.03pp [+0.76pp, +6.62pp] on this corpus, but 7 of 19 committed artefacts show it positively against 12 negatively",
      "holds": true
    }
  ],
  "gates": [
    {
      "id": "V59-ledger-corpus",
      "pass": true,
      "criterion": "the corpus is the one R30 stage 2 paid for: 7,000 questions, 58 databases, 203 rules, 13,578 injections",
      "actual": "7000 questions, 58 databases, 203 rules, 13578 injections, 187 repaired, 237 broken"
    },
    {
      "id": "V60-ledger-split",
      "pass": true,
      "criterion": "the two halves deal 29 databases each, share none, and between them hold every question",
      "actual": "3230 + 3770 questions over 29 databases per half"
    },
    {
      "id": "V61-ledger-family",
      "pass": true,
      "criterion": "the pack-size family was recomputed on at least fifteen committed artefacts and every row has a sign",
      "actual": "19 artefacts: 7 positive, 12 negative, 0 flat"
    }
  ],
  "statement": "Across 203 rules and 13578 injections, a rule's record is 293 against a permutation null of 210 (p = 0.0047), so the rules are not interchangeable — but the record does not travel: measured on one set of databases and again on another it correlates -0.13 over 135 rules with sign agreement of 49%. At 66.9 injections per rule the ledger is mostly its own noise, and a rule that looked harmful does not keep looking harmful where it was not measured (-0.06pp [-1.97pp, +1.56pp]). The one structural variable with a signal is how many rules the prompt carries: 423 questions served a single rule return +3.07pp against -0.96pp for the 6576 served two, a difference of +4.03pp [+0.76pp, +6.62pp] — and that contrast reverses on 12 of the 19 committed artefacts, so it is a corpus effect until an arm says otherwise.",
  "notes": [
    "The unit of the ledger is the injection, not the rule: a question with a two-rule pack credits both rules with the same flip, which is why the dispersion null permutes outcomes rather than resampling rules.",
    "The split-half correlation is the reliability of a half-length ledger; Spearman-Brown turns it into the reliability of the full one, and that number is what a prune has to clear.",
    "The pack-size contrast is not randomized. Retrieval serves one rule when only one clears the threshold, and those questions are harder (47.3% unanswerable by the bare model against 41.4%), so both arms are netted on their own questions and the interval is clustered by database."
  ]
}
